# Agent definition files are hashed byte-for-byte (definition_hash in public.agents,
# computed by scripts/factory-runner/sync-agents.mjs and re-verified at dispatch time by
# scripts/factory-runner/provider.mjs's startRunByAgentId) to detect drift between the
# live on-disk file and what the registry believes was registered. Without this file, a
# checkout's line-ending conversion (core.autocrlf) is environment-dependent - the exact
# same git blob content checks out as LF on one machine/config and CRLF on another (found
# live during independent Phase 5 verification, 2026-08-31: a fresh `git worktree add`
# checkout on this same Windows machine, using the ambient core.autocrlf=true, produced
# CRLF for these files while the long-lived primary working directory - checked out under
# different historical settings - had LF, matching the git blob and the DB's stored hash;
# not a real registry defect since the primary directory content was already correct, but
# a real latent fragility in the hash-based drift-detection mechanism itself for any
# future clone/checkout). Forcing LF here means definition_hash means the same thing
# everywhere this repo is checked out, independent of any individual machine's
# core.autocrlf setting.
.claude/agents/*.md text eol=lf

# run41: the deployed-v92 reference copies must check out BYTE-EXACT on every machine. The committed
# blobs are pure LF and hash to 795c20c82301aba1..., but with no attribute git's autocrlf rewrote them
# to CRLF on checkout (sha 49d53882...), so verifiers #38 and #39 both reported the reference as
# untrustworthy for rollback and worked around it from git instead. They were right about their own
# worktrees and the blob was right too; the file just had no eol attribute.
qa/verification/scratch/v92/index.v92.ts eol=lf
qa/verification/scratch/v92/**/v92.lf.ts eol=lf
qa/verification/scratch/v92/v92.lf.ts eol=lf
